# Captain channel routing: where the bus is, what it carries, and how the captain
# hears about it. bin/jg-bus.sh is the one reader; a home's config/captain-channel
# overrides any single key here, and JG_CAPTAIN_CHANNEL overrides both files.
#
# The channel is in SHADOW: every captain-facing event is recorded and published
# while the paths that reach him today keep running untouched.
#   shadow        on   tee the events; off  do nothing at all.
#   The transport itself is not a key here: bin/jg-bus.sh publishes through the Dapr
#   sidecar, and core/bus/dapr/components/pubsub.yaml picks what the bus really is.
#   nats-url      the server core/bus/nats.conf starts; dapr-http-url/-ports are
#                 the sidecar started beside it.
#   stream        the JetStream stream core/bus/stream.json creates for subject-prefix.
#                 The metric server stays off in this step (see jg-bus.sh serve): a
#                 metrics port is shared machine surface and nothing scrapes it yet.
#   ntfy-deliver  on   also deliver each event to the captain's iPad;
#                 off  (the default) record and publish only. Shadow means nothing
#                      he sees changes until delivery is turned on deliberately.
#   ntfy-relay    the relay switch. off publishes only to ntfy-server, the local
#                 server core/bus/ntfy.yml starts; on would send the same event
#                 through ntfy-relay-server instead. The captain has not answered
#                 the relay question, so this ships off and stays off until he does.
set shadow on
set nats-url nats://127.0.0.1:4222
set dapr-http-url http://127.0.0.1:3500
set dapr-http-port 3500
set dapr-grpc-port 50001
set dapr-app-id juggernaut-bus
set dapr-pubsub captain-channel
set subject-prefix jg.captain
set stream CAPTAIN
set ntfy-server http://127.0.0.1:2586
set ntfy-topic juggernaut-captain
set ntfy-tag juggernaut
set ntfy-deliver off
set ntfy-relay off
set ntfy-relay-server https://ntfy.sh
